 [17592186487277 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487277 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487277 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487277 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487277 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487277 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487277 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186486826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487277 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f77d5683-3c91-57b4-a6c6-baacce64e39e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487283 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487283 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487283 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487283 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487283 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487283 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487283 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487283 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186486930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487283 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fc1b300f-8550-5161-bdfe-afedec77711a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487295 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487295 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148664" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487295 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487295 #edb/ref 1071 "A manufacturing company has three sites, factory $1,$ factory $2,$ and factory $3.$ They all produce two light bulb types: A and B.\n\n- Factory $1$ produces $25%$ of the entire production, and $2%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\n- Factory $2$ produces $50%$ of the entire production, and $3%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\n- Factory $3$ produces $25%$ of the entire production, and $1%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\nIf $40%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, and $1%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, what is the probability that a randomly chosen defective light bulb is of type A? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487295 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1462b070-fed9-586f-924d-db4fb0fa8a6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487295 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ be the event that a light bulb is of type A, $D$ be the event that a light bulb is defective, and ${F}_{1},{F}_{2},{F}_{3}$ be the event that a light bulb is produced in factory $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({F}_{1})=0.25 &  & P(D|{F}_{1})=0.02 \\\\  & P({F}_{2})=0.5 &  & P(D|{F}_{2})=0.03 \\\\  & P({F}_{3})=0.25 &  & P(D|{F}_{3})=0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${F}_{1}⊔{F}_{2}⊔{F}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({F}_{1})+P({F}_{2})+P({F}_{3})=0.25+0.5+0.25=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $40%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since $1%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.01.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen defective light bulb is of type A, which is represented as $P(A|D).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|D) & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D|{F}_{1})P({F}_{1})+P(D|{F}_{2})P({F}_{2})+P(D|{F}_{3})P({F}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.01)(0.4)}{(0.02)(0.25)+(0.03)(0.5)+(0.01)(0.25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.004}{0.0225} \\\\  & ≈0.178,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487306 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487306 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147889" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487306 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487306 #edb/ref 1071 "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{2}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=0.75, &  \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0.15, & P({A}_{2})=0.3 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=0.8, & P({A}_{3})=0.6\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487306 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "25486da2-4aed-57cd-8065-4f05b0f0cbe6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487306 #edb/ref 1074 "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=3,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}) & =1-(P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})) \\\\  & =1-(0.3+0.6) \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{2}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.15)(0.3)}{(0.75)(0.1)+(0.15)(0.3)+(0.8)(0.6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.045}{0.6} \\\\  & =0.075.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487319 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487319 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148270" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487319 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487319 #edb/ref 1071 "An English teacher believes that students who prefer print handwriting tend to perform better in essay writing. To prove her belief, she puts some students into three groups according to their average writing grades and asks them if they usually write in print.\n\n- $20%$ of students have low grades.\n\n- $65%$ of students have average grades, and $55%$ of those students usually write in print.\n\n- $15%$ of students have high grades, and $52%$ of those students usually write in print.\n\nIf $42%$ of students are female, $80%$ of female students write in print, and $56%$ of students that write in print are female, what is the probability that a randomly chosen student with low grades writes in print? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487319 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42158c74-7958-54df-8e89-80fc77114095" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487319 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $W$ be the event that a student writes in print, and $L,A,H$ be the event that a student has low, average, or high writing grades, respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(L)=0.2 \\\\  & P(A)=0.65 &  & P(W|A)=0.55 \\\\  & P(H)=0.15 &  & P(W|H)=0.52\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union $L⊔A⊔H$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP(L)+P(A)+P(H)=0.2+0.65+0.15=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $42%$ of students in the entire school are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.42.\n$$\n\n- Since $80%$ of females write in print, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|F)=0.8.\n$$\n\n- Since $56%$ of students that write in print are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F|W)=0.56.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student with low grades writes in print, which is represented as $P(W|L).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\ P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|L)P(L)+P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|H)P(H)} \\\\ 0.56 & =\\frac{(0.8)(0.42)}{P(W|L)(0.2)+(0.55)(0.65)+(0.52)(0.15)} \\\\ 0.56 & =\\frac{0.336}{0.2P(W|L)+0.4355}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(W|L),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.56(0.2P(W|L)+0.4355) & =0.336 \\\\ 0.2P(W|L)+0.4355 & =\\frac{0.336}{0.56} \\\\ 0.2P(W|L)+0.4355 & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0.2P(W|L) & =\\frac{3}{5}-0.4355 \\\\ P(W|L) & =\\frac{1}{0.2}(\\frac{3}{5}-0.4355) \\\\ P(W|L) & ≈0.823\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487321 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487321 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487321 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487321 #edb/ref 1071 "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{2}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=\\frac{1}{5}, & P({A}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=\\frac{1}{8}, & P({A}_{2})=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=1, & P({A}_{3})=\\frac{7}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487321 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "43f08f68-330a-59ee-956f-33e7cbacb451" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487321 #edb/ref 1074 "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=3,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{7}{20}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{2}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{8})(\\frac{2}{5})}{(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{1}{4})+(\\frac{1}{8})(\\frac{2}{5})+(1)(\\frac{7}{20})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{20})}{(\\frac{9}{20})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487323 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487323 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148667" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487323 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487323 #edb/ref 1071 "A local newspaper is sold by three distributors, $1,$ $2,$ and $3.$\n\n- Distributor $1$ buys $33%$ of the copies, and $90%$ of those copies are sold.\n\n- Distributor $2$ buys $25%$ of the copies, and $87%$ of those copies are sold.\n\n- Distributor $3$ buys $42%$ of the copies, and $82%$ of those copies are sold.\n\nIf $5%$ of all copies have some printing defect, and $70%$ of defective copies are sold, what is the probability that a randomly chosen sold copy has a printing defect? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487323 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "47b23978-faf0-57d0-85ed-6efe7d74f281" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487323 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $R$ be the event that a copy has a printing defect, $S$ be the event that a copy is sold, and ${D}_{1},{D}_{2},{D}_{3}$ be the event that a copy is bought by distributor $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({D}_{1})=0.33 &  & P(S|{D}_{1})=0.9 \\\\  & P({D}_{2})=0.25 &  & P(S|{D}_{2})=0.87 \\\\  & P({D}_{3})=0.42 &  & P(S|{D}_{3})=0.82\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${D}_{1}⊔{D}_{2}⊔{D}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({D}_{1})+P({D}_{2})+P({D}_{3})=0.33+0.25+0.42=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $5%$ of all copies has some printing defect, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.05.\n$$\n\n- Since $70%$ of defective copies are sold, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|R)=0.7.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen sold copy has a printing defect, which is represented as $P(R|S).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S|{D}_{1})P({D}_{1})+P(S|{D}_{2})P({D}_{2})+P(S|{D}_{3})P({D}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.7)(0.05)}{(0.9)(0.33)+(0.87)(0.25)+(0.82)(0.42)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.035}{0.8589} \\\\  & ≈0.041,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487326 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487326 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148152" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487326 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487326 #edb/ref 1071 "Some researchers want to test the belief that teenagers who play an instrument are more likely to be good at math. They collect data on a large sample of teenagers that play an instrument.\n\n- $30%$ of teenagers play piano, and $50%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\n- $20%$ of teenagers play violin, and $60%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\n- $50%$ of teenagers play other instruments, and $47%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\nIf $10%$ of teenagers started to play music before $6$ years old, and $58%$ of teenagers who started to play before $6$ years old are good at math, what is the probability that a randomly chosen teenager that is good at math started to play music before $6$ years old? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487326 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "521e5a18-fa22-58cf-9f82-7b4aa14a1323" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487326 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $B$ be the event that a teenager started to play music before $6$ years old, $M$ be the event that a teenager is good at math, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a teenager plays piano, violin, or another instrument, respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(M|{T}_{1})=0.5 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.2 &  & P(M|{T}_{2})=0.6 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.5 &  & P(M|{T}_{3})=0.47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.3+0.2+0.5=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of teenagers started to play before $6$ years old, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.1.\n$$\n\n- Since $58%$ of students who started to play before $6$ years old are good at math, we have\n\n$$\nP(M|B)=0.58.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen teenager who is good at math started to play before $6$ years old, which is represented as $P(B|M).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|M) & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(M|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(M|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.58)(0.1)}{(0.5)(0.3)+(0.6)(0.2)+(0.47)(0.5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.058}{0.505} \\\\  & ≈0.115,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487345 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487345 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147886" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487345 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487345 #edb/ref 1071 "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{1}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=0, & P({A}_{1})=0.25 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0.6, & P({A}_{2})=0.25 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=0.3, & P({A}_{3})=0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487345 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "855a7cb7-0048-5462-8815-114b332521cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487345 #edb/ref 1074 "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=3,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})=0.25+0.25+0.5=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)(0.25)}{(0)(0.25)+(0.6)(0.25)+(0.3)(0.5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0.3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487354 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487354 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148523" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487354 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487354 #edb/ref 1071 "A company is selling a new product on three e-commerce platforms, platform $1,$ platform $2,$ and platform $3.$\n\n- $44%$ of customers bought the product on platform $1,$ and $70%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\n- $25%$ of customers bought the product on platform $2,$ and $85%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\n- $31%$ of customers bought the product on platform $3.$\n\nIf $25%$ of all customers are teenagers, $60%$ of teenage customers are satisfied with the product, and $20%$ of satisfied customers are teenagers, what is the probability that a randomly chosen customer from platform $3$ is satisfied? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487354 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9b22d29b-922e-508b-b541-5acf3087f769" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487354 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $T$ be the event that a customer is a teenager, $S$ be the event that a customer is satisfied, and ${P}_{1},{P}_{2},{P}_{3}$ be the event that a customer bought the product on platform $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({P}_{1})=0.44 &  & P(S|{P}_{1})=0.7 \\\\  & P({P}_{2})=0.25 &  & P(S|{P}_{2})=0.85 \\\\  & P({P}_{3})=0.31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${P}_{1}⊔{P}_{2}⊔{P}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({P}_{1})+P({P}_{2})+P({P}_{3})=0.44+0.25+0.31=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $25%$ of all customers are teenagers, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $60%$ of teenage customers are satisfied, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|T)=0.6.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of satisfied customers are teenagers, we have\n\n$$\nP(T|S)=0.2.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen customer from platform $3$ is satisfied with the product, which is represented as $P(S|{P}_{3}).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T|S) & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S)} \\\\ 0.2 & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S|{P}_{1})P({P}_{1})+P(S|{P}_{2})P({P}_{2})+P(S|{P}_{3})P({P}_{3})} \\\\ 0.2 & =\\frac{(0.60)(0.25)}{(0.7)(0.44)+(0.85)(0.25)+P(S|{P}_{3})(0.31)} \\\\ 0.2 & =\\frac{0.15}{0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(S|{P}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2(0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3})) & =0.15 \\\\ 0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.15}{0.2} \\\\ 0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 0.31P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4}-0.5205 \\\\ P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.31}(\\frac{3}{4}-0.5205) \\\\ P(S|{P}_{3}) & ≈0.740\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487355 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487355 #edb/ref 1069 "q-170685" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487355 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487355 #edb/ref 1071 "A farm sells its apples to three grocery stores; $1,$ $2,$ and $3.$\n\n- Store $1$ buys $14%$ of the apples.\n\n- Store $2$ buys $26%$ of the apples, and $50%$ of those apples are sold in one day.\n\n- Store $3$ buys $60%$ of the apples, and $25%$ of those apples are sold in one day.\n\nIf $63%$ of all apples are red, $25%$ of red apples are sold in one day, and $45%$ of apples sold in one day are red, what is the probability that a randomly chosen apple bought by store $1$ is sold in one day? Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487355 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9c74ed7c-bf09-54d6-a2a8-5c3ba0194b49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487355 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $R$ be the event that an apple is red, $S$ be the event that the apple is sold in one day, and ${S}_{1},{S}_{2},{S}_{3}$ be the event that an apple is sold by store $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({S}_{1})=0.14 \\\\  & P({S}_{2})=0.26 &  & P(S|{S}_{2})=0.5 \\\\  & P({S}_{3})=0.60 &  & P(S|{S}_{3})=0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}⊔{S}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})+P({S}_{3})=0.14+0.26+0.60=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $63%$ of all apples are red, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.63.\n$$\n\n- Since $25%$ of red apples are sold in one day, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|R)=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $45%$ of apples sold in one day are red, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|S)=0.45.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen apple bought by store $1$ is sold in one day, which is represented as $P(S|{S}_{3}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S)} \\\\ P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S|{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(S|{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})+P(S|{S}_{3})P({S}_{3})} \\\\ 0.45 & =\\frac{(0.63)(0.25)}{P(S|{S}_{1})(0.14)+(0.5)(0.26)+(0.25)(0.60)} \\\\ 0.45 & =\\frac{0.1575}{0.14P(S|{S}_{1})+0.28}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(S|{S}_{1}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.45(0.14P(S|{S}_{1})+0.28) & =0.1575 \\\\ 0.14P(S|{S}_{1})+0.28 & =\\frac{0.1575}{0.45} \\\\ 0.14P(S|{S}_{1})+0.28 & =0.35 \\\\ 0.14P(S|{S}_{1}) & =0.35-0.28 \\\\ 0.14P(S|{S}_{1}) & =0.07 \\\\ P(S|{S}_{1}) & =\\frac{0.07}{0.14} \\\\ P(S|{S}_{1}) & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487364 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487364 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487364 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487364 #edb/ref 1071 "An English teacher believes that students who prefer cursive handwriting tend to perform better in essay writing. To prove his belief, he puts some students into groups of three according to their average writing grades and asks them if they usually write in cursive.\n\n- $20%$ of students have low grades, and $12%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\n- $65%$ of students have average grades, and $15%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\n- $15%$ of students have high grades, and $17%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\nIf $56%$ of students are female, and $16%$ of female students write in cursive, what is the probability that a randomly chosen student who writes in cursive is a female? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487364 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a806821e-50df-52c7-a557-34976fdac21e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487364 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $W$ be the event that the student writes in cursive, and $L,A,H$ be the events that the student has low, average, or high grades, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(L)=0.2 &  & P(W|L)=0.12 \\\\  & P(A)=0.65 &  & P(W|A)=0.15 \\\\  & P(H)=0.15 &  & P(W|H)=0.17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union $L⊔A⊔H$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP(L)+P(A)+P(H)=0.2+0.65+0.15=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $56%$ of students are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.56.\n$$\n\n- Since $16%$ of female students write in cursive, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|F)=0.16.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student who writes in cursive is a female, which is represented as $P(F|W).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|L)P(L)+P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|H)P(H)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.16)(0.56)}{(0.2)(0.12)+(0.65)(0.15)+(0.15)(0.17)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.0896}{0.147} \\\\  & ≈0.610,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487367 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487367 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487367 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487367 #edb/ref 1071 "Some researchers want to test the belief that teenagers that play an instrument are more likely to be good at math. They collect data on a large sample of teenagers that play an instrument.\n\n- $20%$ of teenagers play the piano, and $60%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\n- $15%$ of teenagers play the violin.\n\n- $65%$ of teenagers play other instruments, and $35%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\nIf $12%$ of teenagers started to play music before $6$ years old, $27%$ of teenagers who started to play before $6$ years old are good at math, and $8%$ of students who are good at math started to play an instrument before $6$ years old, what is the probability that a randomly chosen teenager that plays the violin is good at math? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487367 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ad4190f4-bdf3-5375-9c0a-f6bde72a3f1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487367 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $B$ be the event that a teenager started to play music before $6$ years old, $M$ be the event that a teenager is good at math, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a teenager plays piano, violin, or another instrument, respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.2 &  & P(M|{T}_{1})=0.6 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.15 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.65 &  & P(M|{T}_{3})=0.35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.2+0.15+0.65=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $12%$ of teenagers started to play before $6$ years old, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.12.\n$$\n\n- Since $27%$ of teenagers who started to play before $6$ years old are good at math, we have\n\n$$\nP(M|B)=0.27.\n$$\n\n- Since $8%$ of students who are good at math started to play an instrument before $6$ years old, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|M)=0.08.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen teenager who plays the violin is good at math, which is represented as $P(M|{T}_{2}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|M) & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M)} \\\\ P(B|M) & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(M|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(M|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\ 0.08 & =\\frac{(0.27)(0.12)}{(0.2)(0.6)+P(M|{T}_{2})(0.15)+(0.35)(0.65)} \\\\ 0.08 & =\\frac{0.0324}{0.3475+0.15P(M|{T}_{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(M|{T}_{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.08(0.3475+0.15P(M|{T}_{2})) & =0.0324 \\\\ 0.3475+0.15P(M|{T}_{2}) & =\\frac{0.0324}{0.08} \\\\ 0.3475+0.15P(M|{T}_{2}) & =\\frac{81}{200} \\\\ 0.15P(M|{T}_{2}) & =\\frac{81}{200}-0.3475 \\\\ P(M|{T}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{0.15}(\\frac{81}{200}-0.3475) \\\\ P(M|{T}_{2}) & ≈0.383\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487380 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487380 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147887" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487380 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487380 #edb/ref 1071 "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}⊔{A}_{4}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3},{A}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{1}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=\\frac{1}{5}, &  \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0, & P({A}_{2})=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=\\frac{1}{5}, & P({A}_{3})=\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{4})=\\frac{2}{5}, & P({A}_{4})=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487380 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c768c6df-9213-55c1-bcb4-06af864aa6e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487380 #edb/ref 1074 "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=4,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}⊔{A}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})+P({A}_{4})=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}) & =1-(P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})+P({A}_{4})) \\\\  & =1-(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})+P(B|{A}_{4})P({A}_{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{3}{8})}{(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{3}{8})+(0)(\\frac{1}{4})+(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{1}{8})+(\\frac{2}{5})(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{40})}{(\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487402 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487402 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148085" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487402 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487402 #edb/ref 1071 "A gambling room has three roulette tables, table $1,$ table $2,$ and table $3.$ Data of all bets of a particular evening are collected.\n\n- $30%$ of bets were played at table $1,$ and $5%$ of those bets were won.\n\n- $35%$ of bets were played at table $2,$ and $6%$ of those bets were won.\n\n- $35%$ of bets were played at table $3,$ and $7%$ of those bets were won.\n\nIf $10%$ of bets were played in the first hour, and $4%$ of bets made in the first hour were won, what is the probability that a randomly chosen winning bet was played in the first hour? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487402 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f697c855-b9dd-5b7e-bb24-b23154c897b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487402 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a bet is played in the first hour, $W$ be the event that a bet is won, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a bet is played at table $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(W|{T}_{1})=0.05 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.35 &  & P(W|{T}_{2})=0.06 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.35 &  & P(W|{T}_{3})=0.07\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.3+0.35+0.35=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of bets in the entire evening are played in the first hour, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.1.\n$$\n\n- Since $4%$ of bets made in the first hour are won, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|F)=0.04.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen winning bet is played in the first hour, which is represented as $P(F|W).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(W|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(W|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.04)(0.1)}{(0.05)(0.3)+(0.06)(0.35)+(0.07)(0.35)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.004}{0.0605} \\\\  & ≈0.066,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487403 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487403 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159358" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487403 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487403 #edb/ref 1071 "A travel agency offers three itineraries, itinerary $1,$ itinerary $2,$ and itinerary $3.$ For each itinerary, customers can decide to travel using their own car or the agency's bus.\n\n- Itinerary $1$ is chosen by $60%$ of the customers, and $10%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n\n- Itinerary $2$ is chosen by $20%$ of the customers, and $25%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n\n- Itinerary $3$ is chosen by $20%$ of the customers.\n\nIf $30%$ of all customers travel with children, $50%$ of customers with children choose the bus, and $83%$ of customers that choose the bus travel with children, what is the probability that a randomly chosen customer that chooses itinerary $3$ takes the bus? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487403 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fa54d1c3-840f-55dc-9c9a-7cb1c9435baf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487403 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be the event that a customer has children, $B$ be the event that a customer takes the bus, and ${I}_{1},{I}_{2},{I}_{3}$ be the event that a customer chooses itinerary $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({I}_{1})=0.6 &  & P(B|{I}_{1})=0.1 \\\\  & P({I}_{2})=0.2 &  & P(B|{I}_{2})=0.25 \\\\  & P({I}_{3})=0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${I}_{1}⊔{I}_{2}⊔{I}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({I}_{1})+P({I}_{2})+P({I}_{3})=0.6+0.2+0.2=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $30%$ of customers have children, we have\n\n$$\nP(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $50%$ of customers with children choose the bus, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|C)=0.5.\n$$\n\n- Since $83%$ of customers that choose the bus have children, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|B)=0.83.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen customer that chooses itinerary $3$ travels by bus, which is represented as $P(B|{I}_{3}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C|B) & =\\frac{P(B|C)P(C)}{P(B)} \\\\ P(C|B) & =\\frac{P(B|C)P(C)}{P(B|{I}_{1})P({I}_{1})+P(B|{I}_{2})P({I}_{2})+P(B|{I}_{3})P({I}_{3})} \\\\ 0.83 & =\\frac{(0.5)(0.3)}{(0.1)(0.6)+(0.25)(0.2)+P(B|{I}_{3})(0.2)} \\\\ 0.83 & =\\frac{0.15}{0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(B|{I}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.83(0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3})) & =0.15 \\\\ 0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.15}{0.83} \\\\ 0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{15}{83} \\\\ 0.2P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{15}{83}-0.11 \\\\ P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.2}(\\frac{15}{83}-0.11) \\\\ P(B|{I}_{3}) & ≈0.354\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
